      explanation: "1. GIVEN: P·e^x = Q·e^(-x) FOR ALL REAL VALUES OF x (THIS EQUATION MUST HOLD FOR EVERY POSSIBLE x, NOT JUST SOME SPECIFIC VALUE). 2. RECOGNIZE THAT e^x AND e^(-x) ARE LINEARLY INDEPENDENT FUNCTIONS OF x (NEITHER CAN BE EXPRESSED AS A CONSTANT MULTIPLE OF THE OTHER FOR ALL x — THEY GROW/DECAY IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS AS x VARIES). 3. FOR AN EQUATION OF THE FORM P·f(x) = Q·g(x) TO HOLD FOR ALL x, WHERE f AND g ARE LINEARLY INDEPENDENT FUNCTIONS, THE ONLY WAY THIS CAN BE SATISFIED IS IF BOTH COEFFICIENTS ARE ZERO (P=0 AND Q=0) — OTHERWISE, THE EQUATION WOULD ONLY HOLD FOR SPECIFIC, ISOLATED VALUES OF x (WHERE THE TWO SIDES HAPPEN TO COINCIDE), NOT FOR ALL x. 4. TO VERIFY THIS DIRECTLY: SUBSTITUTE TWO DIFFERENT VALUES OF x AND SOLVE THE RESULTING SYSTEM. AT x=0: P·e^0=Q·e^0, i.e., P=Q (SINCE e^0=1). AT x=1: P·e=Q·e^(-1), i.e., P·e = Q/e, SO P·e² = Q (MULTIPLYING BOTH SIDES BY e). 5. COMBINING P=Q (FROM x=0) WITH P·e²=Q (FROM x=1): SUBSTITUTE Q=P INTO THE SECOND EQUATION: P·e²=P, i.e., P(e²−1)=0. SINCE e²≠1 (e IS NOT ±1), WE MUST HAVE P=0, AND CONSEQUENTLY Q=P=0 AS WELL. 6. So P=Q=0 IS THE ONLY SOLUTION THAT SATISFIES THE EQUATION FOR ALL x, matching option A."
