      q: "Consider a binary tree T in which every node has either zero or two children. Let n > 0 be the number of nodes in T. Which ONE of the following is the number of nodes in T that have exactly two children?",
      options: ["(n-2)/2","(n-1)/2","n/2","(n+1)/2"],
      answer: 1,
      marks: 1,
      explanation: "1. IN A BINARY TREE WHERE EVERY NODE HAS EITHER ZERO CHILDREN (a LEAF) OR EXACTLY TWO CHILDREN (a FULL INTERNAL NODE), THERE IS A STANDARD, WELL-KNOWN RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN THE NUMBER OF LEAVES (n0) AND THE NUMBER OF 2-CHILD (FULL) INTERNAL NODES (n2). 2. LET n0 = NUMBER OF LEAF NODES (0 CHILDREN) AND n2 = NUMBER OF FULL INTERNAL NODES (2 CHILDREN). THE TOTAL NUMBER OF NODES IS n = n0 + n2 (SINCE EVERY NODE IS EITHER A LEAF OR A 2-CHILD NODE, WITH NO OTHER TYPE ALLOWED). 3. USE THE STANDARD IDENTITY FOR SUCH 'FULL' BINARY TREES: n0 = n2 + 1 (THIS FOLLOWS FROM COUNTING EDGES: EACH NODE EXCEPT THE ROOT HAS EXACTLY ONE PARENT EDGE, SO THERE ARE n−1 TOTAL EDGES; EACH 2-CHILD NODE CONTRIBUTES EXACTLY 2 CHILD EDGES, SO TOTAL EDGES = 2×n2 = n−1, GIVING n2 = (n−1)/2, AND CONSEQUENTLY n0 = n−n2 = n−(n−1)/2 = (n+1)/2 = n2+1). 4. SUBSTITUTE n0=n2+1 INTO n=n0+n2: n = (n2+1)+n2 = 2n2+1. 5. SOLVE FOR n2: n2 = (n−1)/2. 6. So THE NUMBER OF NODES WITH EXACTLY TWO CHILDREN IS (n−1)/2, matching option B."
