      q: "Consider an unordered list of N distinct integers. What is the minimum number of element comparisons required to find an integer in the list that is NOT the largest in the list?",
      options: ["1","N - 1","N","2N - 1"],
      answer: 0,
      marks: 1,
      explanation: "1. WE WANT TO FIND AN INTEGER IN THE LIST THAT IS GUARANTEED NOT TO BE THE LARGEST — NOT necessarily THE SMALLEST, JUST SOME ELEMENT THAT IS DEFINITELY NOT THE MAXIMUM. 2. THIS IS a MUCH WEAKER REQUIREMENT THAN FINDING THE ACTUAL MINIMUM OR MAXIMUM (WHICH WOULD REQUIRE N−1 COMPARISONS IN THE WORST CASE, EXAMINING ALL ELEMENTS) — WE JUST NEED TO IDENTIFY ONE ELEMENT THAT IS PROVABLY 'NOT THE LARGEST'. 3. CONSIDER MAKING JUST ONE COMPARISON BETWEEN ANY TWO ARBITRARY ELEMENTS, SAY A[i] AND A[j] (i≠j): THE SMALLER OF THESE TWO ELEMENTS IS GUARANTEED TO NOT BE THE OVERALL LARGEST ELEMENT OF THE ENTIRE LIST — WHY? BECAUSE THE LARGEST ELEMENT OF THE LIST MUST BE ≥ BOTH A[i] AND A[j] (SINCE IT'S THE LARGEST OF EVERYTHING), SO WHICHEVER OF A[i],A[j] IS SMALLER CANNOT BE THE OVERALL MAXIMUM (SINCE THE OTHER ONE, OR POSSIBLY SOME THIRD ELEMENT, IS AT LEAST AS LARGE). 4. THIS MEANS A SINGLE COMPARISON SUFFICES: COMPARE ANY TWO ELEMENTS, AND OUTPUT THE SMALLER OF THE TWO — THIS ELEMENT IS GUARANTEED TO NOT BE THE LARGEST IN THE ENTIRE LIST (SINCE IT LOST TO AT LEAST ONE OTHER ELEMENT IN THE COMPARISON, PROVING IT CANNOT BE THE MAXIMUM OF THE WHOLE SET). 5. So THE MINIMUM NUMBER OF COMPARISONS NEEDED IS 1, matching option A."
