      marks: 1,
      explanation: "1. a DETERMINISTIC PUSHDOWN AUTOMATON (DPDA) IS a RESTRICTED VERSION OF a PUSHDOWN AUTOMATON WHERE, AT EACH STEP, THERE IS AT MOST ONE POSSIBLE TRANSITION (NO NONDETERMINISTIC CHOICES) — THE CLASS OF LANGUAGES ACCEPTED BY DPDAs IS CALLED 'DETERMINISTIC CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGES' (DCFL), WHICH IS a STRICT SUBSET OF THE GENERAL CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGES (SOME CFLs REQUIRE NONDETERMINISM AND CANNOT BE ACCEPTED BY ANY DPDA). 2. CHECK option A ('ANY REGULAR LANGUAGE'): EVERY REGULAR LANGUAGE IS A SPECIAL CASE OF a DETERMINISTIC CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGE (SINCE ANY DFA CAN BE TRIVIALLY CONVERTED INTO a DPDA THAT SIMPLY IGNORES ITS STACK, USING ONLY ITS STATE TRANSITIONS TO SIMULATE THE DFA) — SO EVERY REGULAR LANGUAGE CAN ALWAYS BE ACCEPTED BY SOME DPDA — this STATEMENT IS TRUE (ALWAYS HOLDS). 3. CHECK option B ('ANY CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGE'): THIS IS FALSE — THERE EXIST CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGES (LIKE THE CLASSIC EXAMPLE {ww^R} FOR GENERAL w, OR PALINDROME-RELATED LANGUAGES REQUIRING NONDETERMINISTIC GUESSING OF THE MIDPOINT) THAT CANNOT BE ACCEPTED BY ANY DPDA — NOT ALL CFLs ARE DETERMINISTIC. 4. CHECK option C ('ANY LANGUAGE ACCEPTED BY an NPDA'): THIS IS FALSE FOR THE SAME REASON AS OPTION B — NPDAs (NONDETERMINISTIC PDAs) CAN ACCEPT THE FULL CLASS OF CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGES, WHICH IS STRICTLY LARGER THAN WHAT DPDAs CAN ACCEPT — SO NOT EVERY NPDA-ACCEPTED LANGUAGE IS DPDA-ACCEPTABLE. 5. CHECK option D ('ANY DECIDABLE LANGUAGE'): THIS IS FALSE — DECIDABLE (RECURSIVE) LANGUAGES ARE a MUCH LARGER CLASS THAN CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGES (INCLUDING MANY LANGUAGES THAT AREN'T EVEN CONTEXT-FREE AT ALL, LET ALONE DETERMINISTIC CONTEXT-FREE) — DPDAs CANNOT ACCEPT ARBITRARY DECIDABLE LANGUAGES. 6. So ONLY option A ('ANY REGULAR LANGUAGE') IS GUARANTEED TO BE ACCEPTED BY SOME DPDA, matching the answer."
