      answers: [3],
      kind: "msq",
      marks: 1,
      explanation: "1. THE INSTRUCTION SET ARCHITECTURE (ISA) DEFINES THE 'PROGRAMMER-VISIBLE' INTERFACE BETWEEN SOFTWARE AND HARDWARE — THIS INCLUDES THINGS LIKE THE INSTRUCTION SET, ADDRESSING MODES, DATA TYPES, AND THE REGISTER SET (HOW MANY REGISTERS ARE VISIBLE/USABLE BY PROGRAMS) — ANYTHING THAT AFFECTS HOW A PROGRAM IS WRITTEN OR HOW INSTRUCTIONS ARE ENCODED IS PART OF THE ISA. 2. CHECK option A ('THE SIZE OF THE CACHE MEMORY'): CACHE SIZE IS a MICROARCHITECTURAL (IMPLEMENTATION-SPECIFIC) DETAIL — DIFFERENT PROCESSORS IMPLEMENTING THE SAME ISA CAN HAVE DIFFERENT CACHE SIZES WITHOUT AFFECTING PROGRAM COMPATIBILITY OR THE INSTRUCTION SET ITSELF — NOT PART OF the ISA. 3. CHECK option B ('THE CLOCK FREQUENCY'): CLOCK FREQUENCY IS ALSO a PURELY IMPLEMENTATION-SPECIFIC (MICROARCHITECTURAL) DETAIL — DIFFERENT CHIPS IMPLEMENTING THE SAME ISA CAN RUN AT DIFFERENT CLOCK SPEEDS — NOT PART OF the ISA. 4. CHECK option C ('THE NUMBER OF CACHE MEMORY LEVELS'): SIMILARLY, THE CACHE HIERARCHY DESIGN (HOW MANY LEVELS: L1, L2, L3, etc.) IS an IMPLEMENTATION/MICROARCHITECTURAL CHOICE, INVISIBLE TO THE PROGRAMMER'S INSTRUCTION-LEVEL VIEW OF THE MACHINE — NOT PART OF the ISA. 5. CHECK option D ('THE TOTAL NUMBER OF REGISTERS'): THIS IS a DIRECTLY PROGRAMMER-VISIBLE ASPECT OF THE ARCHITECTURE — THE NUMBER OF GENERAL-PURPOSE (OR OTHER) REGISTERS AVAILABLE DIRECTLY AFFECTS HOW INSTRUCTIONS ARE ENCODED (HOW MANY BITS ARE NEEDED TO SPECIFY a REGISTER OPERAND) AND HOW PROGRAMS/COMPILERS CAN USE THE MACHINE (e.g., HOW MANY VALUES CAN BE HELD IN REGISTERS SIMULTANEOUSLY) — THIS IS a DEFINING, PROGRAMMER-VISIBLE PART OF THE ISA. 6. So ONLY 'THE TOTAL NUMBER OF REGISTERS' IS PART OF THE ISA, matching answer [3] (option D)."
