      explanation: "Forward/backward substitution via LY=Q then UX=Y is the standard way to solve PX=Q using an LU decomposition. Since L is unit triangular its determinant is always 1 (always invertible), so det(P) = det(U); hence P invertible implies U (and L) is invertible, and P singular implies some diagonal entry of U is zero. A symmetric P does not in general have a symmetric L and U (that requires the different LDL^T-type decomposition), so the last statement is false."
