      q: "An application executes 6.4 x 10^8 number of instructions in 6.3 seconds. There are four types of instructions: Branch (CPI 2, count 2.25x10^8), Load (CPI 5, count 1.20x10^8), Store (CPI 4, count 1.65x10^8), Arithmetic (CPI 3, count 1.30x10^8). The duration of a clock cycle in nanoseconds is _________. (rounded off to one decimal place)",
      options: [],
      answer: 3,
      tolerance: 0.01,
      kind: "nat",
      marks: 2,
      explanation: "1. COMPUTE THE TOTAL NUMBER OF CLOCK CYCLES USED BY EACH INSTRUCTION TYPE, USING TotalCycles = Σ(CPI_i × Count_i) FOR EACH INSTRUCTION TYPE i. 2. BRANCH: CPI=2, COUNT=2.25×10⁸ — CYCLES = 2×2.25×10⁸ = 4.5×10⁸. 3. LOAD: CPI=5, COUNT=1.20×10⁸ — CYCLES = 5×1.20×10⁸ = 6.0×10⁸. 4. STORE: CPI=4, COUNT=1.65×10⁸ — CYCLES = 4×1.65×10⁸ = 6.6×10⁸. 5. ARITHMETIC: CPI=3, COUNT=1.30×10⁸ — CYCLES = 3×1.30×10⁸ = 3.9×10⁸. 6. SUM ALL CYCLE COUNTS: TOTAL CYCLES = 4.5×10⁸ + 6.0×10⁸ + 6.6×10⁸ + 3.9×10⁸ = (4.5+6.0+6.6+3.9)×10⁸ = 21.0×10⁸ = 2.1×10⁹ CYCLES. 7. COMPUTE THE CLOCK CYCLE TIME: TOTAL EXECUTION TIME / TOTAL CYCLES = 6.3 seconds / (2.1×10⁹ cycles) = 6.3/(2.1×10⁹) SECONDS PER CYCLE. 8. SIMPLIFY: 6.3/2.1 = 3, SO THE CYCLE TIME = 3×10⁻⁹ SECONDS = 3 NANOSECONDS. 9. So THE DURATION OF a CLOCK CYCLE IS 3.0 NANOSECONDS."
